---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="ICP-MS Precision Components & Manufacturing | Maisi Precision"
  description="Explore key manufacturing requirements for ICP-MS precision components, including ion lenses, quadrupole parts, Faraday cups, vacuum chambers, materials, tolerances and cleanliness."
>
  <article>
    <header class="relative overflow-hidden bg-navy-950 text-white">
      <div class="grid-bg absolute inset-0 opacity-20"></div>
      <div class="absolute -right-28 -top-48 h-130 w-130 rounded-full border border-blue-300/20"></div>
      <div class="container-wide relative py-18 md:py-24">
        <p class="mb-6 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[.22em] text-blue-300">Technical guide</p>
        <h1 class="max-w-5xl text-4xl font-semibold leading-[1.07] tracking-[-.045em] sm:text-5xl md:text-6xl">
          Precision Components for ICP-MS Systems: Manufacturing Requirements and Supplier Considerations
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-slate-300">
          A practical overview of precision components across ICP-MS ion transmission, mass analysis, detection, and vacuum sections.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="bg-white py-14 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto w-[calc(100%-2rem)] max-w-3xl">
        <div class="space-y-6 text-[1.02rem] leading-8 text-slate-700">
          <p>Inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) systems rely on a range of precision mechanical components across ion transmission, mass analysis, detection, and vacuum sections.</p>
          <p>For instrument manufacturers, producing these components involves more than machining parts to drawing dimensions. <strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Material selection, dimensional control, surface quality, cleanliness, and vacuum compatibility</strong> all need to be considered according to the function of each component.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-14 space-y-14 md:mt-18 md:space-y-18">
          <section>
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-[-.035em] text-navy-950">1. Why Precision Components Matter in ICP-MS Systems</h2>
            <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 leading-8 text-slate-700">
              <p>Components such as ion lenses, electrodes, quadrupole-related parts, Faraday cups, and vacuum chambers may require precise geometry, alignment, appropriate surface conditions, and careful contamination control.</p>
              <p>A component can meet its nominal dimensions and still be unsuitable for the application if its surface condition, cleanliness, material properties, or vacuum compatibility are overlooked.</p>
              <p>For this reason, ICP-MS component manufacturing requires both <strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">precision machining and an understanding of the component's role within the instrument</strong>.</p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-[-.035em] text-navy-950">2. Typical Precision Components in ICP-MS Systems</h2>
            <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 leading-8 text-slate-700">
              <p>Depending on the instrument design, precision components may include:</p>
              <ul class="space-y-3 border-l-2 border-blue-200 pl-6">
                <li><strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Ion lenses and electrode components</strong> — used to guide, focus, and control ions within the instrument.</li>
                <li><strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Quadrupole components</strong> — precision parts associated with mass filtering, where geometry and alignment can be particularly important.</li>
                <li><strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Faraday cups</strong> — detector-related components requiring precise geometry and reliable integration with surrounding assemblies.</li>
                <li><strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Vacuum chambers and related components</strong> — chambers, interfaces, connectors, and other parts designed for controlled vacuum environments.</li>
                <li><strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Insulators and custom mechanical components</strong> — including ceramic, PEEK, metal, and other application-specific parts used throughout the instrument.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>Because these components perform different functions, their manufacturing requirements should be evaluated individually rather than using the same machining criteria for every part.</p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-[-.035em] text-navy-950">3. Key Manufacturing Requirements</h2>
            <div class="mt-8 space-y-10">
              <section>
                <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-navy-950">Dimensional Accuracy</h3>
                <div class="mt-3 space-y-4 leading-8 text-slate-700">
                  <p>Critical geometry, spacing, alignment, mating features, and sealing interfaces may require tight and repeatable dimensional control.</p>
                  <p>Maisi Precision can machine applicable critical features to tolerances <strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">as tight as ±0.005 mm</strong>, depending on material, geometry, and drawing requirements.</p>
                </div>
              </section>
              <section>
                <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-navy-950">Surface Quality</h3>
                <div class="mt-3 space-y-4 leading-8 text-slate-700">
                  <p>Burrs, scratches, tool marks, roughness, and the condition of critical surfaces can affect assembly, cleanliness, sealing, and subsequent finishing processes.</p>
                  <p>Maisi routinely achieves <strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Ra 0.8 μm</strong>, with <strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Ra 0.4 μm achieved on previous projects</strong>, depending on the component and surface requirements.</p>
                </div>
              </section>
              <section>
                <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-navy-950">Materials</h3>
                <p class="mt-3 leading-8 text-slate-700">Materials used in ICP-MS components vary according to their mechanical, electrical, thermal, insulating, and vacuum functions. Maisi has machining experience with <strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">304/316L stainless steel, aluminum alloys, oxygen-free copper, titanium alloys, ceramics, and PEEK</strong>.</p>
              </section>
              <section>
                <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold leading-snug text-navy-950">Cleanliness and Vacuum Compatibility</h3>
                <div class="mt-3 space-y-4 leading-8 text-slate-700">
                  <p>Components operating within or near the vacuum system require appropriate control of machining oils, particles, residues, and handling contamination.</p>
                  <p>Maisi applies dedicated cleaning processes for high-vacuum and mass spectrometry components according to material, geometry, and customer requirements.</p>
                  <p>For more detail, read <a href="/resources/uhv-precision-components" class="focus-ring font-medium text-navy-800 underline decoration-blue-300 underline-offset-4 hover:text-navy-700">What Makes a Precision Component Suitable for Ultra-High Vacuum (UHV) Applications?</a></p>
                </div>
              </section>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-[-.035em] text-navy-950">4. What to Look for in an ICP-MS Component Manufacturer</h2>
            <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 leading-8 text-slate-700">
              <p>When evaluating a manufacturing partner, instrument manufacturers should consider more than a supplier's stated CNC tolerance.</p>
              <p>Important factors include:</p>
              <ul class="space-y-3 border-l-2 border-blue-200 pl-6">
                <li>Experience manufacturing mass spectrometry components</li>
                <li>Control and inspection of critical dimensions</li>
                <li>Experience with metals, ceramics, and engineering polymers</li>
                <li>Surface finishing and cleanliness control</li>
                <li>Appropriate inspection and verification</li>
                <li>Support for prototypes, small batches, and repeat production</li>
              </ul>
              <p>For complex ICP-MS components, the key question is whether a supplier can <strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">consistently manufacture, inspect, clean, and deliver the component according to its actual application requirements</strong>.</p>
              <p>For broader supplier evaluation guidance, read <a href="/resources/how-to-choose-mass-spectrometry-parts-manufacturer" class="focus-ring font-medium text-navy-800 underline decoration-blue-300 underline-offset-4 hover:text-navy-700">How to Choose a Precision Parts Manufacturer for Mass Spectrometry Systems</a>.</p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-[-.035em] text-navy-950">5. Maisi Precision's ICP-MS Manufacturing Experience</h2>
            <div class="mt-6 space-y-5 leading-8 text-slate-700">
              <p>Maisi Precision has extensive experience manufacturing custom mechanical components for ICP-MS and other mass spectrometry systems.</p>
              <p>Our ICP-MS manufacturing experience includes <strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">electrode components, ion lenses, Faraday cups, quadrupole rods and related components, vacuum chambers, insulating parts, and other custom mechanical components used throughout the instrument</strong>.</p>
              <p>Capabilities include:</p>
              <ul class="space-y-3 border-l-2 border-blue-200 pl-6">
                <li>Tolerances as tight as <strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">±0.005 mm</strong> for applicable critical features</li>
                <li><strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Ra 0.8 μm routinely</strong>, with <strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Ra 0.4 μm achieved on previous projects</strong></li>
                <li>304/316L stainless steel, aluminum alloys, oxygen-free copper, titanium alloys, ceramics, and PEEK</li>
                <li>Grinding, mirror polishing, electropolishing, passivation, anodizing, and plating according to project requirements</li>
                <li>Dedicated cleaning processes for high-vacuum and mass spectrometry components</li>
                <li>Inspection and verification according to drawing and acceptance requirements</li>
              </ul>
              <p>Maisi supports <strong class="font-semibold text-navy-950">prototypes, small batches, and recurring production</strong> based on customer drawings and technical specifications.</p>
            </div>
          </section>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="bg-navy-800 text-white">
    <div class="container-wide flex flex-col gap-7 py-12 md:flex-row md:items-center md:justify-between">
      <div>
        <p class="mb-3 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[.2em] text-blue-200">Discuss your project</p>
        <h2 class="max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-.035em] md:text-4xl">Looking for a Manufacturing Partner for ICP-MS Components?</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-7 text-blue-100">Send us your drawing or 3D model, material specification, critical tolerances, surface requirements, and estimated quantity for manufacturing review.</p>
      </div>
      <a href="/contact#quote" class="focus-ring inline-flex shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-white px-7 py-4 font-semibold text-navy-950 transition hover:bg-blue-50">
        Request a Quote <span class="ml-3" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
